WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:02.570
It's a pleasure to welcome to the program
today.

00:00:02.570 --> 00:00:09.680
Rachel piter Copper, who is an election forecaster
and analyst and a senior fellow at the Niskanen

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:10.680
Center.

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:12.240
Rachel, it's so great to have you on.

00:00:12.240 --> 00:00:13.610
Yeah, it's great to be here.

00:00:13.610 --> 00:00:14.670
Thanks for having me.

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:15.800
So let's get right into it.

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:20.470
So I've sort of intuitively suspected for
some time that at least there's a strategy

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:22.360
for winning elections.

00:00:22.360 --> 00:00:27.130
Convincing people to change their minds about
who they're going to vote for is less efficient

00:00:27.130 --> 00:00:31.960
than just finding people that agree with you
and getting them to go out and vote.

00:00:31.960 --> 00:00:37.749
And you wrote a piece a little while ago where
you are, as I understand it, essentially supporting

00:00:37.749 --> 00:00:42.440
the idea of a turnout strategy rather than
rather than a debate and change people's minds

00:00:42.440 --> 00:00:45.390
strategy being a key to winning elections.

00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:50.159
Am I correctly interpreting your perspective
and talk a little bit about how you formed

00:00:50.159 --> 00:00:51.159
this idea?

00:00:51.159 --> 00:00:52.159
Yeah.

00:00:52.159 --> 00:00:56.600
I mean, you are and it's it's precipice on
this idea of a changed electorate.

00:00:56.600 --> 00:00:57.600
Right.

00:00:57.600 --> 00:01:02.190
So this idea that in the age of polarization,
it's exceedingly difficult to get people to

00:01:02.190 --> 00:01:08.740
change their minds and that we have a much
better and more sophisticated understanding

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:14.070
of electoral behavior and voter opinions than
we did back in the days.

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:16.740
It certainly doesn't advocate for an either
or.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:23.850
I'm not a either or black or white individual
in any capacity of my life or my research.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:27.390
So I'm not saying you ignore persuasion.

00:01:27.390 --> 00:01:33.280
I would argue it's probably most effective
amongst that small group of pure independents,

00:01:33.280 --> 00:01:38.790
even probably would argue its efficacy against
like if you're a Democrat, trying to get an

00:01:38.790 --> 00:01:43.100
independent that leans towards Republicans
to vote for you would be a stretch, let alone

00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:46.369
a self-identified Republican.

00:01:46.369 --> 00:01:50.400
But so I'm not saying you shouldn't do persuasion
at all.

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:55.829
But, you know, until recently, Democrats pretty
much engaged in almost entirely and devoting

00:01:55.829 --> 00:02:02.789
their resources towards persuading spending
geo TV money and their ad strategy and their

00:02:02.789 --> 00:02:10.390
messaging and framing over this idea of convincing
voters of the other party or voters that don't

00:02:10.390 --> 00:02:12.750
agree with them to vote for them.

00:02:12.750 --> 00:02:19.180
And I am arguing that they neglected to consider
shaping the electorate in their image the

00:02:19.180 --> 00:02:21.190
way that the Republicans have.

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:26.100
How have the Republicans done it and what
sorts of strategies work or can you use to

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.280
do that?

00:02:27.280 --> 00:02:28.280
Yeah.

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:34.670
So the way that Republicans have done it primarily
is through effective, you know, research based

00:02:34.670 --> 00:02:36.820
messaging strategies.

00:02:36.820 --> 00:02:42.950
So when you look at how a Republican run,
a Republican run campaign talks to voters

00:02:42.950 --> 00:02:48.150
like they kind of meet the voter where they
are, and you can't say that Democrats are

00:02:48.150 --> 00:02:49.730
as good at that.

00:02:49.730 --> 00:02:53.750
And it's not a flattering place where the
American voter is.

00:02:53.750 --> 00:02:58.730
Unfortunately, Americans are not particularly
civically minded or engaged.

00:02:58.730 --> 00:03:05.070
Even the part that is engaged in elections
tend to be pretty poorly informed.

00:03:05.070 --> 00:03:11.090
So, you know, Democrats tend to have an idealized
version of voters.

00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:17.370
They tend to think that voters want a very
wonky policy based discussion, you know, and

00:03:17.370 --> 00:03:21.900
hit them with these types of mailers that
are, you know, pointing out, well, Republicans

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:26.430
want to do this with, you know, abortion funding
and we want to do that, whereas Republicans

00:03:26.430 --> 00:03:28.440
are talking to voters emotions.

00:03:28.440 --> 00:03:32.690
And the reason that they do that, number one,
is because people are better motivated by

00:03:32.690 --> 00:03:33.690
emotions.

00:03:33.690 --> 00:03:38.270
But number two, you know, if you were talking
to somebody who doesn't have a great understanding

00:03:38.270 --> 00:03:41.900
of policy, it's probably cause they're not
much interested in it.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:42.900
Right.

00:03:42.900 --> 00:03:47.740
And so if you are hitting them with these
wonky, you know, heavily word laden policy

00:03:47.740 --> 00:03:50.210
appeals, they're going to tune out pretty
quickly.

00:03:50.210 --> 00:03:55.550
You want to hit them in a way that resonates
with them and conveys that information pretty

00:03:55.550 --> 00:03:56.550
quick.

00:03:56.550 --> 00:03:59.850
So Republicans really do certain techniques.

00:03:59.850 --> 00:04:05.830
I'm happy to get into specific details about
that are much more effective at getting through

00:04:05.830 --> 00:04:10.140
to that less inclined, less civically engaged
voter.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:16.010
Now, my instinct is that this works better
with some issues than with others in the sense

00:04:16.010 --> 00:04:20.820
that there are issues that naturally lend
themselves more to a more sort of visceral.

00:04:20.820 --> 00:04:24.120
I've got to do something about that sort of
reaction.

00:04:24.120 --> 00:04:28.130
Is that also a part of it, which is figuring
out what is a voting issue in the first place?

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:30.310
Yeah, I mean, that's true, right?

00:04:30.310 --> 00:04:33.120
I mean, to some degree you can't.

00:04:33.120 --> 00:04:36.050
To some degree you can't create salience.

00:04:36.050 --> 00:04:40.240
But I'll push back at that a little bit, too,
because I mean, think about Benghazi.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:41.240
Right.

00:04:41.240 --> 00:04:42.240
Yeah.

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.690
How many Republicans did you run into in 2016?

00:04:44.690 --> 00:04:50.480
That would point to especially the ones that
were like little bit less Trump like, would

00:04:50.480 --> 00:04:51.480
you?

00:04:51.480 --> 00:04:52.480
Trump?

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:53.580
And they would say, well, yeah, but Benghazi.

00:04:53.580 --> 00:04:54.580
Right.

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:55.580
I mean just the.

00:04:55.580 --> 00:04:57.750
They could name Benghazi as a.

00:04:57.750 --> 00:05:03.910
Main issue is a testament to how much you
can actually push and mold public opinion

00:05:03.910 --> 00:05:07.180
and voter like salience if you are effective
at it.

00:05:07.180 --> 00:05:08.880
And you know what you're doing.

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:13.880
Because I wonder, I wonder, was the Benghazi
refrain actually about Benghazi?

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:18.560
Or was it the emotion of being repelled by
Hillary and simply finding something to say

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:19.560
about it?

00:05:19.560 --> 00:05:20.560
Yeah.

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:21.560
I mean, yes and no.

00:05:21.560 --> 00:05:23.330
But like me, it's such a knish issue.

00:05:23.330 --> 00:05:28.370
But yet I still I still run into people who
say, well, I voted for Trump, but I do because

00:05:28.370 --> 00:05:30.850
of Benghazi.

00:05:30.850 --> 00:05:34.970
Like Democrats would tell you that such a
thing would be impossible to do.

00:05:34.970 --> 00:05:35.970
Right.

00:05:35.970 --> 00:05:37.270
So you know, so.

00:05:37.270 --> 00:05:40.510
So the answer is you need to have the right
issue.

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:42.560
It needs to be an issue that resonates with
people.

00:05:42.560 --> 00:05:45.360
But they you need to talk about it in the
right way.

00:05:45.360 --> 00:05:52.040
So like Democrats will talk about health care,
but they don't make it about life and death.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:53.360
I mean, sometimes they do.

00:05:53.360 --> 00:05:59.290
And I think they're getting better about conveying,
you know, if Medicaid expansion doesn't pass,

00:05:59.290 --> 00:06:02.870
this kid with preexisting conditions is going
to die.

00:06:02.870 --> 00:06:03.870
Right.

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:08.620
That's how Republicans would talk about Medicaid
expansion if we don't pass this thing.

00:06:08.620 --> 00:06:09.710
You're going to die.

00:06:09.710 --> 00:06:14.600
And Democrats don't want to take it to that
level of stakes.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:15.600
Right.

00:06:15.600 --> 00:06:17.850
But that's how Republicans.

00:06:17.850 --> 00:06:19.720
Everything is life or death.

00:06:19.720 --> 00:06:24.090
If you don't vote for a Republican in twenty
nineteen in Virginia, they're going to take

00:06:24.090 --> 00:06:25.090
your guns away.

00:06:25.090 --> 00:06:29.970
It's not they're gonna make modest reforms
to the gun laws that make it so that you have

00:06:29.970 --> 00:06:33.240
additional hurdles to clear for a gun purchase.

00:06:33.240 --> 00:06:36.770
It's you know, they're going to take all your
guns.

00:06:36.770 --> 00:06:42.520
And so it is a two fold problem that Democrats
have identified the right issues.

00:06:42.520 --> 00:06:45.610
And frankly, sometimes they're decent at that.

00:06:45.610 --> 00:06:52.080
But talking about them in a way that for you
and me, work campaigns are not about us.

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:58.180
Campaigns are about those people that don't
give a poop, about politics, about policy.

00:06:58.180 --> 00:07:04.320
And the reason is that nobody is explained
to them how important that policy is to them

00:07:04.320 --> 00:07:05.320
personally.

00:07:05.320 --> 00:07:07.480
The stakes that those things have for them.

00:07:07.480 --> 00:07:14.220
And, you know, we I would say or I would argue
Democrats tend to make campaigns that are

00:07:14.220 --> 00:07:18.750
ready, designed to talk to voters that I care
about politics.

